<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AssemblyName>axcall</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Axcall</RootNamespace>
    <!-- Baseline version for local/dev builds; the release workflow
         overrides this with -p:Version from the git tag. -->
    <Version>0.2.1</Version>
    <!-- No ICU. axcall is invariant-culture throughout by design (callsigns
         are upper-cased with ToUpperInvariant, every number is parsed with
         InvariantCulture), so it loses nothing, and a self-contained publish
         then needs no libicu on the target. That is what lets the .deb declare
         a short Depends and run on a bare base image. -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Publish strategy. Measured at 0.4.0 on linux-x64:

         plain                74.4 MB   23 ms to start
         trimmed              15.7 MB   52 ms
         trimmed + R2R        21.5 MB   21 ms
         NativeAOT             3.9 MB    4 ms

       Trimming alone starts more than twice as slowly, because the runtime pack
       ships its framework assemblies ReadyToRun-compiled and trimming rewrites
       them, discarding that native code. NativeAOT wins on both counts, so it
       is used everywhere it can be built, which means everywhere the release
       has a native runner: Linux x64 and arm64, macOS, Windows.

       linux-arm (armhf) is the exception and keeps trimming plus ReadyToRun.
       NativeAOT has no armhf runner to build on and cannot be cross-linked to
       armhf from x86_64, and armhf is the one target where that is an
       acceptable answer. It is still 71% smaller than an untrimmed publish,
       with no startup cost.

       TrimmerSingleWarn=false lists every trim and AOT warning rather than one
       summary. With TreatWarningsAsErrors from Directory.Build.props that turns
       any future reflection the compiler cannot follow into a build failure,
       which is what keeps both strategies safe. There are none today. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != '' and '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != 'linux-arm'">
    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != '' and '$(PublishAot)' != 'true'">
    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <PublishReadyToRun>true</PublishReadyToRun>
    <PublishSingleFile>true</PublishSingleFile>
    <IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>true</IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''">
    <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Axcall.Core\Axcall.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Packet.Core" />
    <PackageReference Include="Packet.Ax25" />
    <PackageReference Include="Packet.Kiss" />
    <PackageReference Include="Packet.Kiss.Serial" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Axcall.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
